 \paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker gain within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm0.05$; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first, and the classification is established for these six markets, not for the neighbourhood. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain, so given newest-first targeting the priority phase is close to redundant. (iii) In a registered diagnostic, R3 keeps auditing an honest provider's replacement identity well after its window, at a measured cost in that provider's profit per round: the targeting that works against the attacker also burdens honest newcomers. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
