\paragraph{Identity replacement and the B3--R3 gap (exploratory follow-up).} D2+H3 is D2 plus one switch $x$ that lets the attacker leave after a failed audit and return under a new identity. At the headline market, the D2+H3 search leaves \site{rs_hl_b3} more gain against B3 than the D2 search, and \site{rs_hl_r3} more against R3, so the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_hl_g1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_hl_g0} under D2. To see whether this holds on the six fresh markets, we ran after the report the D2 cells that budget cut~\const{3} had removed (B3, R3, and C$^*$ at $\rho=\const{0.2}$, each with a doubling check), under a plan and expectations written before any fresh D2 result existed, but after the headline contrast and the six fresh-market D2+H3 outcomes had been seen. This is a descriptive follow-up, not an independent replication, and it changes no registered decision. Pooled over the six markets with equal weights, the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_g1} (\const{95}\% paired seed-bootstrap interval [\site{rs_g1_lo}, \site{rs_g1_hi}]) under D2+H3 and \site{rs_g0} ([\site{rs_g0_lo}, \site{rs_g0_hi}]) under D2 (doubled searches: \site{rs_g1_d} and \site{rs_g0_d}). The share of the gap that disappears without replacement, $S = \const{1} - G(\mathrm{D2})/G(\mathrm{D2{+}H3})$, is \site{rs_s} (doubled searches: \site{rs_s_d}). It exceeds one because the pooled D2 gap is slightly negative, but that gap is not distinguishable from zero: it is positive on \site{rs_gd0_pos} of the six markets (\site{rs_gd0_pos_d} with the doubled searches), the exploratory $t$ interval over the six market values is [\site{rs_gd0_t_lo}, \site{rs_gd0_t_hi}], and most of its negative value comes from fresh5 (without fresh5, $S$ is \site{rs_s_loo5}). The seed intervals above hold the six markets fixed; leaving out one market at a time gives $S$ from \site{rs_loo_lo} to \site{rs_loo_hi} over both search versions, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-reset} lists the gaps per market. The same holds for the Q1 contrast: the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 gain difference is \site{rs_cr1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_cr0} under D2 ($t$ interval [\site{rs_cr0_lo}, \site{rs_cr0_hi}]; doubled searches [\site{rs_cr0_dlo}, \site{rs_cr0_dhi}]). The difference between the two prescribed searches, $I = g(\mathrm{D2{+}H3}) - g(\mathrm{D2})$, is \site{rs_i_b3} against B3, \site{rs_i_r3} against R3, and \site{rs_i_c} against C$^*$; it does not identify the optimal value of replacement. The expectations set in advance score as follows. $I(\mathrm{B3}) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_b}'' (\site{rs_sign_b} markets with the frozen searches, \site{rs_sign_bd} with the doubled ones). $I(\mathrm{C}^*) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_c}'' (\site{rs_sign_c} and \site{rs_sign_cd} markets; fresh3 gives \site{rs_f3_c} with the frozen searches). Pooled $S > \const{0.5}$: ``\site{rs_exp_s}''. Within the D2 family, at the fixed parameters and search budgets, most of the estimated B3 minus R3 gap under the prescribed searches disappears when the attacker cannot replace its identity. The two searched winners can differ in every knob, not only $x$. With every other knob held fixed, switching replacement off in the D2+H3 winner lowers the gain by \site{rs_fx_b3} against B3 and \site{rs_fx_r3} against R3, and switching it on in the D2 winner raises the gain by only \site{rs_rev_b3} and \site{rs_rev_r3}. No D2 winner replaces its identity. The truthful references can: \site{rs_ref_any} of the \site{rs_ref_n} frozen references replace an identity on the evaluation seeds. Each rule's reference cancels in $I$ but not in $G$. Disabling replacement in every reference changes no reference's profit by more than \site{rs_ref_max}, shifts the pooled gaps by \site{rs_ref_dg}, and leaves $S$ at \site{rs_ref_s} (exploratory check on the Mac, which reproduces the stored reference profits on average to within \site{rs_ref_val}, not seed for seed). $S$ is a ratio of two searched gaps and carries their search error (Table~\ref{tab:ph-reset}). \site{rs_flag} of the \site{rs_flag_n} D2 doubling checks are flagged (largest change \site{rs_flag_max}), and on fresh0 the D2 gap moves from \site{rs_f0_g0} to \site{rs_f0_g0_d} under doubling; an unflagged cell is not a converged one. With the frozen searches, two cells violate the nesting of D2 in D2+H3: $I$ is \site{rs_nest_c} for C$^*$ on fresh3 and \site{rs_nest_r} for R3 on fresh4. The doubled searches remove both, so for the C$^*$ contrast the doubled value is the more reliable one.
\begin{table}[t]\centering\small
